pointed us to how important social relationships are for our well-being. And he showed that all of the people who indicate on a one to ten scale that they're at the most happy 10, all of them to a person indicate having a close friend in their lives. And it's research that like that that's really kept me going because time and time again we see how important our relationships are for how happy we are and increasingly we're finding out for how healthy we are too. I once gave a talk about five years or so was an interdisciplinary conference, a small meeting. And the person before me was an exercise physiologist. And at the beginning of their talk, they said, I challenge anyone to argue with me about the importance of physical activity. You can't overstate the importance of physical activity.
向我们指明了社会关系对我们的幸福感有多么重要。他发现，所有在一到十分的量表上标示自己处于最快乐的“10分”的人，无一例外地都表明他们的生活中有一个密友。正是这样的研究一直激励着我，因为我们一次又一次地看到，我们的关系对我们的快乐程度有多么重要，而且我们越来越发现，它对我们的健康状况也同样重要。大约五年前，我在一个跨学科会议（一个小会议）上作了一次演讲。排在我前面的那个人是一位运动生理学家。在演讲开始时，他们说：“我向任何人发起挑战，来跟我争论体力活动的重要性。你无论如何强调体力活动的重要性都不为过。”

6
00:03:39.000 --> 00:04:23.000
And I came up and I talked next. And I said, I don't mean to try to top you, but. And then I showed her a graph of basically all of the different things that are associated with longevity, including not. smoking, including physical activity, including not being a, you know, fairly low weight and things like that. And right at the top was our social relationships. And it's a pretty robust finding that people who are more socially connected live longer. And there are all kinds of fascinating studies. There's a great study that you might have heard of called the nuns study, where they studied these nuns over several decades.
然后我上台进行了接下来的演讲。我说：“我并不是想压倒你，但是……”接着我向她展示了一张图表，图表列出了基本上所有与长寿相关的不同因素，包括不吸烟、包括体力活动、包括体重不太重等等。而排在最顶端的正是我们的社会关系。这是一个相当稳健的发现，即社会联系更多的人寿命更长。这里有各种各样引人入胜的研究。有一个你可能听说过的伟大的研究，叫做修女研究（Nuns Study），他们对这些修女进行了几十年的研究。

7
00:04:23.000 --> 00:05:12.000
And nuns are a great group to study because they all live in the same environment, physical environment. So you're controlling for a lot of extremist factors. And by a mile, the nuns who had the closest relationships lived the longest, and the nuns who are the happiest live the longest. And we've seen this in the Harvard Men's study, you know, that's been going on for close to 100 years now. And so it's really these kind of, you know, the early work in this area that to me was so compelling that got me to shift from just studying the basics of what makes relationships tick and what makes relationships succeed and fail to really how they're, they are tied to why we are happy and healthy.
修女是一个很好的研究群体，因为她们都生活在相同的环境中，即物理环境。因此你控制了很多混杂因素。毫无疑问，关系最亲密的修女活得最久，最快乐的修女也活得最久。我们在哈佛大学男性研究（Harvard Men's Study）中也看到了这一点，你知道，这项研究已经持续了近100年。因此，确实是这一类早期研究（在我看来是如此引人注目）促使我从仅仅研究关系运转的基本原理、关系成败的原因，转向真正研究它们是如何与我们为何快乐健康联系在一起的。

