and we did what's called a meta-analysis or an analysis of many papers, all the papers that have been done at the time, this is about five years ago, on the links between marital satisfaction and longevity. And when you look at other studies that have looked at just social connectedness in terms of how integrated you are with your social network, the social network findings are actually bigger. So although your marriage, if you are married, really impacts your physical health, those other close relationships that we have are enormously important to. And I say close relationships because those are the ones that we've studied like marriage, friendships to a lesser extent, parent-child relationships to a greater extent. Not really, one of the things I've become interested
我们做了一个所谓的元分析（Meta-analysis），即对当时许多论文（关于婚姻满意度与长寿之间联系的所有已发表论文）进行的分析，这大约是五年前的事。当你观察其他研究时，那些仅仅从社会网络整合度（即你与社会网络的融合程度）角度来考察社会联系的研究，其社会网络的研究结果实际上更大。因此，尽管你的婚姻（如果你已婚的话）确实会极大地影响你的身体健康，但我们拥有的其他亲密关系也同样极其重要。我之所以说“亲密关系”，是因为这些是我们研究过的关系，比如婚姻、在较小程度上的友谊、在更大程度上的亲子关系。其实，我在疫情期间变得感兴趣的一件事

10
00:06:37.000 --> 00:07:07.000
in actually during the pandemic is the importance of not. non-close relationships, you know, that's something that we've, a lot of us have lost. But yes, absolutely. Friendships, for example, just matter so much. Ed Deiner showed us that. We've done some podcasts where we've talked to people about the importance of even sort of ships in the night kinds of relationships where you may just have a conversation with a stranger and how you derive benefits from that. So let me ask you, then, what are the biological mechanisms that underlie the link between
实际上是非亲密关系的重要性，你知道，那是我们很多人都失去的东西。但是的，绝对的。比如友谊，就非常重要。埃德·迪纳向我们展示了这一点。我们做过一些播客，跟人们讨论过哪怕是那种“萍水相逢”式的关系的重要性，即你可能只是和陌生人进行了一次对话，以及你是如何从中获得益处的。那么，请允许我问你，那么，联系背后的生物学机制是什么？

