00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:45.000
relationships and health? You talk about relationships get under our skin. What do you mean by that? So that's a tougher question to answer, in part because the science is changing so quickly on what we call biomarkers in health psychology, those specific physiological markers that we can assess that seem to, on the one hand, robustly predict physical health outcomes like asthma morbidity, for example, cardiovascular functioning, risk of heart attack, and that are also associated with relationships.
关系与健康之间的关系？你谈到“关系深入我们的骨髓”，你这是什么意思？这是一个更难回答的问题，部分原因是健康心理学中所谓的生物标志物（biomarkers）科学发展得太快了，那些我们可以评估的特定生理标记，它们一方面似乎能够强有力地预测身体健康结果，例如哮喘发病率、心血管功能、心脏病发作风险，另一方面也与关系有关。

12
00:07:45.000 --> 00:08:18.000
And if you look at the history of health psychology, you can see if you take a snapshot of, say, 40 years ago, the hot thing was like heart rate and blood pressure. And then it starts moving toward different markers of the immune system 20 or 30 years ago, some of Sheldon Cohen's work, the researcher who studied the links between stress and the common cold, where he starts looking at. at what we call pro-inflammatory cytokines, which are markers of inflammation in the immune system. We start seeing studies of cortisol. So we're looking at, we've looked at all of these things.
如果你回顾健康心理学的发展史，你会发现如果你截取比如40年前的一个时间断面，最热门的话题是心率和血压。然后，在20或30年前，它开始转向免疫系统的不同标志物，比如谢尔顿·科恩（Sheldon Cohen）的一些研究（这位研究压力与普通感冒之间联系的研究人员），他开始关注我们所说的促炎细胞因子（pro-inflammatory cytokines），它们是免疫系统中炎症的标志物。我们开始看到关于皮质醇（cortisol）的研究。所以我们一直在观察所有这些事情。

13
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:54.000
And I would say the most compelling evidence for relationships linking health are mortality findings. And you see that time and time again. Mortality is pretty easy to measure. Whereas when you're measuring something like pro-inflammatory cytokines from a one-shot blood draw, Our cytokine levels fluctuate greatly across the day. And so it's a much less precise measure. Having said that, we have found more positive social relationships in kids. We've looked at we have a big study that's been going on for about 10 years in Detroit,
我想说，关系与健康相联系的最令人信服的证据是死亡率的研究。你一次又一次地看到这一点。死亡率相对容易测量。然而，当你通过单次抽血来测量像促炎细胞因子这样的指标时，我们的细胞因子水平在一天当中会剧烈波动。因此，这是一个精确度低得多的测量指标。话虽如此，我们在儿童身上发现了更多积极的社会关系。我们观察过——我们在底特律进行了一项历时约10年的大型研究，

14
00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:44.000
Michigan of youth with asthma. And we've shown that positive parent-child relationships are associated with lower levels of inflammation in these youth and that those levels of inflammation aren't in turn associated with better asthma symptoms. We've looked a lot at the stress hormone cortisol, which is naturally secreted throughout the day, does a lot of things, primarily metabolic function, but is a good measure of stress. We have some pretty consistent evidence that positive social relationships are associated with a steeper decline in cortisol across the day. We'll have people, we've done a few studies like this where you give people a little cotton swabs to take home with them. And over a long
密歇根州患有哮喘的青少年。我们已经证明，积极的亲子关系与这些青少年较低的炎症水平相关，而这些炎症水平反过来又与更好的哮喘症状相关。我们对压力激素皮质醇进行了大量研究，它在全天自然分泌，具有多种功能，主要是代谢功能，但它也是衡量压力的一个很好的指标。我们有一些相当一致的证据表明，积极的社会关系与全天皮质醇水平更陡峭的下降有关。我们会让人们——我们做过一些这样的研究，你给人们一些小棉签让他们带回家。在漫长的

15
00:09:44.000 --> 00:10:32.000
weekend, from say a Friday through a Monday, six times a day, they'll chew on a little cotton swab at predetermined times to capture the circadian rhythm of cortisol. And then on those individual days, we'll either ask them about what their relationships are like, or we also use this device that's that's an app on Android and an iPhone called the electronically activated recorder or the ear to sort of take a fly on the wall perspective of people's social interactions which is a great objective way of tapping into relationships and either way you look at it if it's self-reports daily reports or through the ear through sound files that are coded by our research assistance you find these same kinds of effects with sort of quote
周末里，比如从星期五到星期一，一天六次，他们在预定的时间咀嚼一根小棉签，以捕捉皮质醇的昼夜节律。然后在那些日子里，我们要么询问他们关于他们的关系是什么样的，要么我们还使用一种设备，它是安卓和iPhone上的一个应用程序，叫做电子激活记录仪（electronically activated recorder，简称EAR），从“墙上苍蝇”的角度来观察人们的社会社交互动，这是深入了解关系的一个极好的客观方法。无论你如何看待它，如果是自我报告、每日报告，或是通过电子激活记录仪捕捉由我们的研究助手编码的声音文件，你都会发现这些相同类型的效应，即所谓更健康的皮质醇模式与更健康的婚姻功能相关。

16
00:10:32.000 --> 00:11:23.000
healthier cortisol patterns being associated with healthier relationship functioning. But are those things going to be the holy grail of our understanding of why relationships are associated with health? I doubt it. But they all point in the same direction. Showing that relationships are associated with health-related physiology. And I think one of the most exciting things about this line of research that keeps me interested, is that we're learning more and more every year about how this works from a biological standpoint. So not all marriages are good ones, of course, and bad marriages can also have an effect on our health. What are the differences between those kinds of relationships that lead to better health and well-being and those that don't?
但是，这些东西会成为我们理解为什么关系与健康有关的“圣杯”吗？我对此表示怀疑。但它们都指向同一个方向，表明关系与健康相关的生理学有关。我认为这项研究最令人兴奋的事情之一、也是让我保持兴趣的事情，是我们每年都在从生物学的角度更多地了解这是如何运作的。当然，并非所有的婚姻都是美好的，糟糕的婚姻也会对我们的健康产生影响。那些能够带来更好健康和幸福感的关系与不能的关系，它们之间有什么区别？

17
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:58.000
So that's a good question because, you know, there's this general finding that being married is good for your health. And that is on the whole true. But if you dig deeper, it's much better to not be married for your health and well-being than it is to be in a bad marriage. And so you're asking what constitutes sort of, you know, the negative sorts of aspects of marriage that we're looking at that are associated with poor health. The biggest thing is conflict and how people have conflict. Much of our work is in line with some of the classic studies in clinical and social psychology
这是一个很好的问题，因为，你知道，有一个普遍的发现，即结婚对你的健康有益。总的来说，这确实是真的。但如果你深入挖掘，为了你的健康和幸福感，不结婚其实比身处一段糟糕的婚姻要好得多。所以你问的是，我们所关注的、与健康状况不佳相关的婚姻中消极方面的成分究竟是什么？最大的因素是冲突以及人们如何产生冲突。我们的许多工作都符合临床和社会心理学的一些经典研究，

18
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:40.000
that John Gottman and others did in the lab where they found that levels of hostility in conflict discussions and low levels of positive emotions in trying to resolve conflict are associated with poor health, a former graduate student of mine who's now a professor, Sabrina Byerstedel, just published a paper last year looking at this where we had couples interacting in the lab trying to resolve a long-standing disagreement. Of course, they never resolve it. But the point is to try to see how they try to resolve it. And so we found exactly what she had predicted,
比如约翰·戈特曼（John Gottman）和其他人在实验室里所做的研究，他们发现冲突讨论中的敌对程度高、以及在试图解决冲突时积极情绪水平低，都与健康状况不佳有关。我的一位前研究生、现在是一名教授的萨布丽娜·拜尔斯泰德尔（Sabrina Byerstedel），去年刚刚发表了一篇论文，探讨了这个问题。我们在实验室里让伴侣们互动，试图解决一个长期的分歧。当然，他们从未真正解决它。但重点是看他们是如何试图解决它的。因此我们发现了完全符合她预测的结果，

19
00:12:40.000 --> 00:13:25.000
which was that these hostile behaviors that come out in these interactions among some couples were associated after they left the lab with dysregulated cortisol patterns in daily life, with a less, steep decline in cortisol throughout the day. In other words, the cortisol stayed high for those couples. So I would say conflict is the big one, but also a lack of positive emotions, too. You know, those intimate relationships that are lacking humor, that are lacking, caring, and that are lacking one big thing that I've looked at throughout my almost 20 years doing this
即在某些伴侣的这些互动中表现出的这些敌对行为，在他们离开实验室后，与日常生活中的皮质醇失调模式有关，表现为全天皮质醇下降幅度较小。换句话说，对于这些伴侣来说，皮质醇保持在高位。所以我想说，冲突是最大的一个因素，但缺乏积极情绪也是。你知道，那些缺乏幽默感、缺乏关怀、并且缺乏我在近20年的关系研究中一直关注的一个重大因素的亲密关系，

20
00:13:25.000 --> 00:14:09.000
research and relationships. And that is what we call perceived partner responsiveness. This is a really, really important thing for relationships, not just for your own physical health and well-being, but especially for the health of the relationship. And so the idea here is that partners vary in the extent to which they make each other feel understood, really listened to, cared for, validated. You know, it's not just being a good listener, but that's a big part of it. It's also being responsive to your partner's needs, being sensitive, you know, knowing when they might need what we call instrumental support or, you know,
那就是我们所说的感知到的伴侣响应性（perceived partner responsiveness）。这对关系来说是一件非常非常重要的事情，不仅对你自己的身体健康和幸福感很重要，对关系的健康尤其重要。所以这里的观点是，伴侣在多大程度上让他们彼此感到被理解、被真正倾听、被关怀、被认可，这是因人而异的。你知道，这不仅仅是做一个好的倾听者，尽管这是其中很大一部分。它还包括对伴侣的需求做出响应，保持敏感，你知道，知道他们什么时候可能需要我们所说的工具性支持（instrumental support），或者，

21
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:52.000
taking out the garbage, helping clean the kitchen, help take the kids somewhere, being sensitive to those kinds of things. Those positive aspects of responsiveness we found are really important in terms of predicting health. A former postdoc of mine, Sarah Stanton, had a really nice paper published a couple of years ago in the journal Psychosomatic Medicine where she showed over a 20-year period from a large data set called Midlife in the United States that's at the University of Wisconsin publicly available data of a few thousand people that perceived partner responsiveness at the very beginning of the study predicted 10 years later how people were responding.
