00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:48.000
People who have been getting outside more during the pandemic are a lot happier. People who are exercising more are happier. And not surprisingly, relationships play a key role, especially face-to-face one. So for those people that were able to have pods or, you know, small groups of people that they, that they were comfortable seeing during the pandemic, those in-person interactions were associated with boosts and happiness and lower levels of depression during the pandemic. Spending time with your kids can be both a stressor during the pandemic, but also can give people a great sense of meaning. Zoom was an interesting thing that we've been looking at. That was one of those things I would never have predicted at the very beginning. of the pandemic would end up being one of these things that we studied. But we found the smaller
在疫情期间经常去户外的人要快乐得多。多锻炼的人更快乐。毫无疑问，关系起到了关键作用，特别是面对面的关系。因此，对于那些能够在疫情期间拥有社交泡沫（pods）或他们感到舒适可以见面的小群体的那些人来说，那些面对面的互动与幸福感的提升以及疫情期间较低的抑郁水平相关。花时间陪孩子在疫情期间既可能是一个压力源，但也能够给人们带来巨大的意义感。Zoom是我们一直在研究的一件有趣的事情。那是我在疫情刚开始时绝对预测不到会成为我们研究对象的事情之一。但我们发现，较小的

41
00:28:48.000 --> 00:29:36.000
Zoom calls, the smaller video chats seem to be the most gratifying to people, that they feel more connected after a one-on-one because it much more approximates an in-person social interaction than does a large Zoom call. The problem with a large video chat, even though they can have their merit certainly and people have had these Zoom happy hours and things like that. But it's not a natural happy hour, right? Like at a happy hour, you're not going to be sitting around to the circle of people going, okay, you talk next, you go next, you go next, right? You're going to be pairing off maybe with one or two people over here. You know, maybe you say, okay, I'm going to go get another drink because the conversation was boring. And then, you know, you have, it's much more
Zoom通话，即较小的视频聊天，似乎对人们最有满足感，他们在进行了一对一聊天后会感到更多的连接，因为它比大型Zoom通话要更接近面对面的社会互动。大型视频聊天的问题在于，尽管它们当然有其价值，人们也举行过这类Zoom欢乐时光（happy hours）之类的活动。但它并不是一场自然的欢乐时光，对吧？就像在欢乐时光里，你不会围成一圈人，然后说：“好的，接下来你讲，下一个你讲，下一个你讲，”对吧？你可能会在这里和一两个人结成小对子。你知道，也许你会说：“好吧，我要去拿杯饮料，因为这个对话有点无聊。”然后，你知道，你会有，它要

42
00:29:36.000 --> 00:30:27.000
organic, right? And it allows much more of a give and take. Whereas online, it's really, you know, there's a lot of listening, hopefully, and just a little bit of talking. And so it's, that's why we think that the small ones are much more like the conversation you and I are having. That's much more akin to a real world conversation. And one of the more interesting things from our Zoom findings was that extroverts in particular loathe large Zoom calls because they can't talk. So they don't have the opportunity like they would at a party to really to have that social need met. And also, they can't be responded to. I said before, the importance of responsiveness. And it's really hard to feel responded to in a large Zoom call
有机得多，对吧？它允许更多的给予和接受。而在网上，实际上，你知道，会有很多倾听（希望如此），只有一点点说话。所以这就是为什么我们认为小的聚会更像你和我正在进行的对话。那更类似于现实世界的对话。从我们的Zoom研究中得到的最有趣的事情之一是，外向者特别讨厌大型Zoom通话，因为他们无法说话。所以他们不像在派对上那样有机会真正满足那种社会需求。此外，他们无法得到回应。我之前说过响应性的重要性。在大型Zoom通话中，真的很难感受到被回应，

43
00:30:27.000 --> 00:31:02.000
because people are distracted, you don't know if they're really paying attention to you. The cues are not there like they would be in real life. So that's kind of interesting, too. Another finding that we had was about extroversion. This reminds me, if you spend any time on Twitter, which I have from time to time, there were a lot of memes early last year about look out for your extroverted friends. They're really suffering right now. And from everything that I know as a psychologist about extroversion, I thought, I don't think that's right.
因为人们分心了，你不知道他们是否真的在关注你。线索并不像在现实生活中那样存在。所以这也是一件很有趣的事情。我们的另一个发现与外向性有关。这让我想起，如果你花任何时间在Twitter上——我有时会这样做——去年年初有很多模因（memes）都在说：照顾好你的外向者朋友。他们现在真的在受苦。而根据我作为心理学家对外向性的所有了解，我想：我觉得这不对。

44
00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:47.000
I think extroverts are generally probably doing better right now. Extroverts tend to have more energy. They tend to find creative solutions to social problems in terms of like making sure those social needs get met, whereas introverts will often in a situation like the pandemic not have the inclination to sort of make the effort to stay socially connected. And they tend to be a little bit lower in energy too. so you're not going to see people going outside as much. And that's actually exactly what we found was that over the first three months of the pandemic, the most extroverted people showed the fewest depressive symptoms. They showed the highest levels of happiness and meaning in life. And we found that that was at least partially explained by feeling more socially connected
我认为外向的人目前总体上可能过得更好。外向的人往往有更多的精力。他们倾向于为社交问题寻找创造性的解决方案，比如确保这些社交需求得到满足，而内向的人在疫情这样的情况下，往往没有倾斜度去努力保持社交联系。而且他们的精力也往往稍低一些，所以你不会看到人们经常去户外。而这实际上正是我们所发现的，在疫情的前三个月里，最外向的人表现出的抑郁症状最少。他们表现出最高水平的幸福感和生活意义。我们发现，这至少部分可以通过感到更多的社交联系来解释

45
00:31:47.000 --> 00:32:20.000
with other people compared to their more introverted counterparts. And also doing things like exercising more, getting outside more, All those things that we already know during the pandemic were good for people's well-being. So, yeah, lots of interesting findings. We have a big, it's a big international consortium at this point of a bunch of researchers working on this project. And so we seem to have new findings every week. One of the things we were about to look at is long-distance relationships during the pandemic, which we know very little about how distance may have impacted people.
与更内向的人相比，他们与其他人的交往更多。也做一些事情，比如多锻炼，多去户外，所有这些我们在疫情期间就已经知道的事情都有益于人们的福祉。所以，是的，有很多有趣的发现。在这一点上，我们有一个大型的国际联合研究小组，由许多研究人员组成，他们都在进行这个项目。因此，我们似乎每周都有新的发现。我们要研究的事情之一是疫情期间的远程恋爱，对于距离如何影响人们，我们所知甚少。

46
00:32:20.000 --> 00:33:13.000
Well, another area where you've done some researchers looking at cross-cultural relationships, I know you worked with couples in Japan. What similarities or differences did you find in terms of how close relationships affect health and well-being in different cultures? So the project that I told you about where we followed, where we had data for couples over 20 years, this midlife in the United States study in Wisconsin, it turns out that there is a corollary study to it in Japan that has all of the exact same measures. age-matched with the Wisconsin sample. So it offers a really nice opportunity for researchers to look at differences between two pretty different cultures. On the one hand, the United States, which is what we would call a more independent culture, a more individualistic culture, and then Japan on the
嗯，你在其中做过一些研究的另一个领域是跨文化关系，我知道你曾与日本的伴侣合作过。在亲密关系如何影响不同文化的健康和福祉方面，你发现了哪些相似之处或不同之处？所以我跟你讲过的那个项目，我们追踪了伴侣长达20年的数据，即威斯康星州的中年美国人研究，结果发现日本有一个与它相对应的研究，其中包含了完全相同的测量指标。与威斯康星州的样本年龄相匹配。因此，它为研究人员提供了一个非常好的机会，来观察两个截然不同的文化之间的差异。一方面是美国，我们可以称之为更独立的文化，一种更具个人主义的文化，而日本在

47
00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:55.000
other, which is a more collectivistic or interdependent culture. And so this emerged out of a graduate course on social relationships that I teach, where we were talking about responsiveness And one of the PhD students in the class said, you know, Rich, you keep talking about how important responsiveness is in people's intimate relationships. But I lived in Japan for a few years. And the outside relationships, outside the intimate relationships. So relationships with family members especially seem to be a lot more important. And she said, I just think that responsiveness is going to matter less for people's happiness there than it would here.
另一方面，这是一个更具集体主义或相互依赖的文化。这源于我教授的一门关于社会关系的毕业生课程，我们在课上讨论了反应性。班上的一名博士生说，你知道，里奇，你一直在谈论反应性在人们的亲密关系中有多么重要。但我曾在日本生活过几年。外在的关系，在亲密关系之外的关系。因此，与家庭成员的关系似乎要重要得多。她说，我只是认为，在那里，反应性对人们幸福感的重要性将不如在这里重要。

48
00:33:55.000 --> 00:34:40.000
And so she did some preliminary analyses and I bumped into a very close colleague at a meeting who I've published a few papers on responsiveness with. And I told him about this idea and he said, you're not going to believe it. We're looking at that exact same question. And so we joined forces to look at this. And what we found was, as my student initially had thought, there were differences. Now, responsiveness in your relationship still was associated. with greater well-being in Japan, but not to the same extent as it is here in the United States. Here, how responsive your intimate partner is seems to matter a lot more because we put so much weight
于是她做了一些初步的分析，我在一次会议上遇到了一个非常亲密的同事，我曾和他一起发表过几篇关于反应性的论文。我把这个想法告诉了他，他说，你不会相信的。我们正在研究完全相同的问题。于是我们联手来研究这个问题。我们发现，正如我的学生最初所想的那样，确实存在差异。现在，你的关系中的反应性仍然与日本更高的幸福感相关，但没有像在美国这样达到相同的程度。在这里，你的亲密伴侣的反应有多快似乎更重要，因为我们寄予了太多的厚望

49
00:34:40.000 --> 00:35:48.000
on our intimate relationships. Eli Finkel has done some really nice work in this area, the social psychologist at Northwestern University, who's written a book called The All or Nothing marriage. And he has hypothesized that in Western society, over the last 100 years or so, the intimate relationship has been almost an exclusive focus for us in terms of, for those that are in intimate relationships for pathways to happiness and meaning in life. In other words, we are expecting more and more from our intimate partners in terms of helping us to be happy. And for those marriages that are incredibly happy and bring us meaning, people seem to be highly self-actualized and to use terms of Abraham Maslow. Whereas those couples where they're not getting those needs, self-esteem needs and those kinds of needs, you know, about that deal with the core self, those kinds of needs met, that they're miserable at the extremes.
在我们亲密的关系上。伊利·芬克尔（Eli Finkel）在这个领域做了一些非常出色的工作，他是西北大学的社会心理学家，曾写过一本名为《全部或无的婚姻》的书。他假设，在西方社会，在过去的100年左右里，亲密关系几乎成了我们唯一的焦点，对于那些处于亲密关系中的人来说，它是通往幸福和生活意义的途径。换句话说，我们对亲密伴侣的期望越来越高，希望他们能帮助我们获得幸福。对于那些极其幸福并带给我们意义的婚姻来说，人们似乎是高度自我实现的，并使用了亚伯拉罕·马斯洛的术语。而对于那些没有满足这些需求的伴侣来说，自尊需求和那种需求，你知道的，涉及核心自我的需求，没有得到满足，他们在极端情况下会痛苦不堪。

50
00:35:48.000 --> 00:36:33.000
And our data seem to suggest that, yes, that is more true here than in countries like Japan, where there's not as much pressure on intimate partners to satisfy all of your needs. The family, the extended social network is more important there than it is here. And I think there's a lesson to be learned there, you know, that maybe we're putting too much pressure on our relationships to make us happy. So, keying off of that, we've been talking a lot about research and theory. So let me ask a question for listeners who might be interested in more practical advice and wisdom that they can draw from your research. Last question, what are the most important things people can do to improve their relationships and become better partners?
我们的数据似乎表明，是的，这在这里比在日本等国更真实，因为在日本，亲密伴侣满足你所有需求的压力没有那么大。家庭、扩展的社会网络在那里比在这里更重要。我认为这里有一个经验教训，你知道，也许我们在关系上施加了太多的压力来让我们快乐。因此，延续这一点，我们一直在讨论很多关于研究和理论的内容。所以让我为那些可能对更多实用建议和智慧感兴趣的听众问一个问题，这些建议和智慧可以从你的研究中汲取。最后一个问题，人们为了改善关系和成为更好的伴侣，可以做的最重要的事情是什么？

51
00:36:33.000 --> 00:37:05.000
Excellent question to end up. Try to be as responsive a partner as you can. And that starts with being a good listener to your partner. When you come home from work, put the phone down, when your partner makes a bid for your attention, really try to, try to pay attention to them and then more broadly to try to be sensitive to their needs. But I think being a really good listener is a key part of that. One of the things that we know is a good booster for relationships is what we call novel and exciting activities.
这是一个很好的结束问题。试着成为一个尽可能有反应的伴侣。这要从做一个倾听伴侣的好听众开始。当你下班回家时，放下手机，当你的伴侣试图引起你的注意时，真的试着，试着关注他们，然后更广泛地试着对他们的需求敏感。但我认为成为一个真正好的听众是其中的关键部分。我们所知道的对关系的一个很好的促进因素是我们所谓的“新奇和令人兴奋的活动”。

52
00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Try something new together. And there's lots of things that will seem new now that we're coming out of the pandemic that we haven't done for a long time. But go on a hike together. Go on a double date together. or just go on a date to a restaurant you haven't tried. You know, trying new things that are fun can be a boost to people's relationships. For parents out there, and I have some friends that are new parents,
